#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# aggregate

#% text
# `aggregate(ds, specs)` gives a dataset of one row that sums up all rows of `ds` as `specs` says.

#% text
# `aggregate(ds, by, specs)` gives a row for each group of rows with the same key `by`.

#% text
# `aggregate(groups, specs)` gives a row for each dataset of the association `groups`, as `group_by` makes it.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `specs` is a list (or a single one) of `c -> f` (the function `f` of column `c`, named `c`), `name -> [c, f]` (named `name`) and `count` (the number of rows, named count).

#% item
# `f` may be `total`, `mean`, `count`, `min`, `max`, or any function, which receives the list of the column's values.

#% item
# Missing values are skipped: an empty `total` or `count` is 0, an empty `mean`, `min` or `max` is `missing()`.

#% item
# `by` is a column name, a list of column names (one key column each), or a function of the row (the key column is then named key).

#% item
# Groups come in the order of their first row; the key columns come first.

#% item
# Numeric columns are summed from their packed values: exact for integers, compensated for reals.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
shop = dataset([["item", "kind", "price", "stock"], ["pen", "office", 1.5, 40], ["lamp", "home", 24.0, 3], ["ink", "office", 6.0, missing()], ["mug", "home", 8.5, 12]])
aggregate(shop, ["price" -> mean, "stock" -> total, count])
#% output
#= price | stock | count
#= ------+-------+------
#=  10.0 |    55 |     4
#= dataset: 1 row, 3 columns

#% text
# One row per kind:

#% cell
aggregate(shop, "kind", ["price" -> max, "cheapest" -> ["price", min], count])
#% output
#= kind   | price | cheapest | count
#= -------+-------+----------+------
#= office |   6.0 |      1.5 |     2
#= home   |  24.0 |      8.5 |     2
#= dataset: 2 rows, 4 columns

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Any function of the values, here the list itself:

#% cell
aggregate(shop, "kind", "item" -> (xs => xs))
#% output
#= kind   | item
#= -------+----------------
#= office | ["pen", "ink"]
#= home   | ["lamp", "mug"]
#= dataset: 2 rows, 2 columns

#% text
# Grouping by a function of the row:

#% cell
aggregate(shop, r => r["price"] > 5, "item" -> count)
#% output
#= key   | item
#= ------+-----
#= false |    1
#= true  |    3
#= dataset: 2 rows, 2 columns

#% text
# The groups of `group_by`:

#% cell
aggregate(group_by(shop, "kind"), "stock" -> total)
#% output
#= key    | stock
#= -------+------
#= office |    40
#= home   |    15
#= dataset: 2 rows, 2 columns

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `count` of a column counts its values; the count spec counts rows:

#% cell
aggregate(shop, ["stock" -> count, count])
#% output
#= stock | count
#= ------+------
#=     3 |     4
#= dataset: 1 row, 2 columns

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# An output name may appear once:

#% cell
aggregate(shop, "kind", "kind" -> count)
#% output
#! failure(duplicate_column, "kind")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `group_by`, `pivot`, `dataset`, `missing`, `total`, `mean`
